====================================================================================
Signal attenuation vs block width - polynomial or exponential?
====================================================================================
  R=0.6, 400 smearing samples, ratio is per-coordinate normalised
  so that growing n does not inflate the norm by dimension alone.

  --- Heisenberg N=4 (M=16) ---
     n  |g_sm|/sqrt(n)  |g_ex|/sqrt(n)    ratio
  ---------------------------------------------
     1                                   0.9411
     2                                   0.9042
     4                                   0.7810
     6                                   0.7268
     8                                   0.7067
    12                                   0.5671
    16                                   0.4331
  polynomial fit  ratio ~ 1.048 * n^(-0.251)   residual 0.2718
  exponential fit ratio ~ 0.991 * 0.9518^n   residual 0.0880
  -> EXPONENTIAL fits better
  cost ~ (M/n)*exp(2*cR2*n) with cR2=0.0494
  -> optimal block width n* = 1/(2*cR2) = 10.1   (M=16, so n*/M = 0.63)
  -> circuits per gradient at n*: M/n* = 1.58
     n*/M roughly CONSTANT across sizes => circuits/gradient is
     constant and the advantage GROWS with M.
     n* roughly constant across sizes => the advantage is a fixed
     factor. Compare this row against the other problem sizes.

